1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,860
After the Allies invaded Iran, Dear Uncle's fantasies became real.

2
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:10,440
His terror of English revenge had turned into madness,

3
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:14,440
and fear of assassination kept him locked in his room.

4
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:20,380
On his orders the garden was guarded, and no one could come or go at night.

5
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:26,920
He even suspected his loyal Mash Ghasem of being an English spy,

6
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:32,920
and was about to kill him, until Shazdeh Asdollah Mirza talked him out of it.

7
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:37,500
Agha Jan, who now regretted his feud with Dear Uncle,

8
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:43,560
decided with Asdollah Mirza to end the English feud with Dear Uncle.

9
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:50,300
For that, they needed an English representative to take part in peace talks.

10
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:58,120
Dear Uncle Napoleon had no choice but to meet a fate like Napoleon Bonaparte's.

11
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:14,400
Uncle Asdollah, do you think this plan we made for Dear Uncle will work?

12
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:16,590
Pray that it does.

13
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:20,250
You and your father caused this whole mess.

14
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:24,660
Called a provincial upstart, he set out to drive the old man mad.

15
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:26,760
And you hadn't the nerve for San Francisco,

16
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:30,690
so you set off firecrackers and drove the poor man crazy.

17
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:33,470
What a father and son! You make a fine pair.

18
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:37,260
You weren't here today to see how narrowly Sir escaped.

19
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:41,300
No! I won't let Brother, the head of our family,

20
00:03:41,340 --> 00:03:43,840
become everyone's plaything like this.

21
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:45,780
Unfortunately, there's no other way.

22
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:48,820
Either Sir is committed to a mental hospital,

23
00:03:48,860 --> 00:03:49,660
or else...

24
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:51,000
Don't talk nonsense, Asdollah!

25
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:53,800
I'd put a bullet in my own brain

26
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:56,720
before I let Brother be taken to an asylum!

27
00:03:56,760 --> 00:04:00,440
A noble family's hundred-year reputation is no joke!

28
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:02,225
Hello, Uncle.

29
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:05,140
Why bring this up in front of the children?

30
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:07,580
Moment... Colonel,

31
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:09,660
first, this boy is no longer a child.

32
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:11,340
He's a sensible young man.

33
00:04:11,380 --> 00:04:14,100
Second, if disaster was avoided today, it was thanks to him.

34
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:15,490
There you are.

35
00:04:15,540 --> 00:04:18,580
Why can't you see how serious this is?

36
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:21,080
Today Sir nearly killed Mash Ghasem!

37
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:23,080
If he had, they'd have jailed him!

38
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:24,180
We must think of something soon.

39
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:27,590
Do you know what a scandal that would have been?

40
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:34,460
I'm ready to give my life for Brother's well-being,

41
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:37,585
but only if we think more sensibly.

42
00:04:38,860 --> 00:04:44,000
But I doubt Brother will believe the English forgave him so easily.

43
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:46,630
Of course, if we find the right man...

44
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:49,420
We've weighed every angle carefully.

45
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:52,600
That representative first makes harsh demands,

46
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:54,900
then, with our mediation, he slowly softens,

47
00:04:54,940 --> 00:04:56,660
and in the end he agrees.

48
00:04:56,700 --> 00:04:59,900
If Sir doesn't obstruct the English,

49
00:04:59,940 --> 00:05:03,370
he'll send a favorable report up to his superiors.

50
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:07,280
On what pretext will you raise this with Brother?

51
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:11,080
Just say the English suddenly felt like contacting him?

52
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:14,600
No, we'll say the English are doing badly in the war with Germany,

53
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:18,900
so now they want to make peace with local opponents all over the world.

54
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:20,660
Leave persuading Sir to me.

55
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:23,300
I had a long talk with the Sardar today.

56
00:05:23,340 --> 00:05:25,800
The poor man means very well,

57
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:28,380
but he said he can't find an Englishman.

58
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:30,880
So things still aren't settled.

59
00:05:30,920 --> 00:05:33,540
Yes, they are! He said he has an Indian friend,

60
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:36,105
a corporal in the Allied army.

61
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:39,460
The Sardar said he can persuade him

62
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:41,740
to take part in our little show,

63
00:05:41,780 --> 00:05:44,180
in return for some reward, of course.

64
00:05:44,220 --> 00:05:48,780
I doubt Sir would agree to negotiate with an Indian corporal.

65
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:51,240
What does this Indian look like?

66
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:53,460
Can he pass for an Englishman?

67
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:57,080
Forget an Englishman, he couldn't pass for a Baluchi!

68
00:05:57,120 --> 00:06:01,840
The Sardar says he's the color of strong milky coffee, head to toe.

69
00:06:03,820 --> 00:06:06,140
I think we'll have to come up with something else.

70
00:06:06,180 --> 00:06:10,240
Besides, even if you get Brother to negotiate with an Indian,

71
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:12,480
what about his rank?

72
00:06:12,520 --> 00:06:15,500
Brother won't accept anyone below a general.

73
00:06:15,540 --> 00:06:17,380
Rank doesn't matter.

74
00:06:17,420 --> 00:06:21,380
Sir doesn't know English army ranks. We'll say he's a colonel.

75
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:25,280
Have you even talked to Brother himself yet?

76
00:06:25,320 --> 00:06:27,220
I've prepared him a little.

77
00:06:27,260 --> 00:06:28,640
The last time I saw him,

78
00:06:28,680 --> 00:06:31,180
I made up that the English, in every occupied country

79
00:06:31,220 --> 00:06:32,840
and unoccupied one, are scrambling

80
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:35,794
to win their opponents over.

81
00:06:35,844 --> 00:06:37,080
How did he react?

82
00:06:37,120 --> 00:06:38,620
Well, he blustered a lot,

83
00:06:38,660 --> 00:06:40,140
that if the English contacted him,

84
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:42,360
he'd never give in, and so on.

85
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:45,080
But once we carry out our plan, I think

86
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:45,820
he'll agree.

87
00:06:45,860 --> 00:06:48,400
He said he doesn't trust the English one bit,

88
00:06:48,440 --> 00:06:50,820
and if they send a representative to him,

89
00:06:50,860 --> 00:06:53,240
he'll have him disarmed first.

90
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:56,280
He said he'd hide Mash Ghasem behind the curtain with a rifle,

91
00:06:56,320 --> 00:06:58,620
so if the English envoy tried to double-cross him,

92
00:06:58,660 --> 00:07:01,460
Mash Ghasem could strike first and finish him off.

93
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:04,200
I'm afraid this will get us all into trouble.

94
00:07:04,240 --> 00:07:08,680
Say this Indian reaches into his pocket for a handkerchief...

95
00:07:09,324 --> 00:07:13,279
Heaven help us if Sir tells Mash Ghasem to fire!

96
00:07:13,604 --> 00:07:17,134
Do you know what the English would do to us then?

97
00:07:17,184 --> 00:07:20,980
You're right! I think we should let Mash Ghasem in on it too.

98
00:07:21,020 --> 00:07:23,140
Mash Ghasem was in the garden. Shall I go call him?

99
00:07:23,180 --> 00:07:23,980
Not a bad idea.

100
00:07:24,020 --> 00:07:24,820
No, no.

101
00:07:24,860 --> 00:07:25,725
Moment!

102
00:07:26,584 --> 00:07:28,440
He mustn't know we held a meeting.

103
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:32,020
Better we go to him, as if by chance. Come.

104
00:07:32,060 --> 00:07:34,455
Ah, after you, Colonel.

105
00:08:00,844 --> 00:08:02,979
How are you, Mash Ghasem?

106
00:08:03,104 --> 00:08:05,239
At your service, sir.

107
00:08:05,484 --> 00:08:07,554
What are you sitting here for?

108
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:12,600
Look at this pretty flower. It grew all by itself.

109
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:16,700
Don't fret over Sir's quarrel.

110
00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:20,100
Are you upset with Sir?

111
00:08:22,900 --> 00:08:26,350
Honestly, sir, we're still not over the fright.

112
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:28,914
Don't hold today against Sir.

113
00:08:28,964 --> 00:08:30,854
Sir has been ill for a few days.

114
00:08:30,904 --> 00:08:34,374
Honestly, we don't believe in doctors and medicines.

115
00:08:34,424 --> 00:08:37,054
But it seems Sir's blood has run a bit hot.

116
00:08:37,104 --> 00:08:37,964
No.

117
00:08:38,004 --> 00:08:40,454
Sir has been imagining things for a while now.

118
00:08:40,504 --> 00:08:43,974
Didn't you see? Today he nearly killed you over a silly notion.

119
00:08:44,024 --> 00:08:47,244
No sane man would ever accuse you of spying.

120
00:08:47,284 --> 00:08:48,594
Don't you agree?

121
00:08:48,644 --> 00:08:51,474
Honestly, sir, we don't want to contradict you.

122
00:08:51,524 --> 00:08:53,810
But we mustn't let our guard down with these English.

123
00:08:53,860 --> 00:08:56,660
Mash Ghasem, you know yourself it's all nonsense.

124
00:08:56,700 --> 00:08:58,180
Honestly, sir, how would we know?

125
00:08:58,220 --> 00:09:00,500
Didn't you see your Brother's imagining things?

126
00:09:00,540 --> 00:09:01,660
Fine, the English are bad,

127
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:02,500
vile,

128
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:03,540
wicked,

129
00:09:03,580 --> 00:09:07,620
but is it right to say Mash Ghasem's in cahoots with them?

130
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:09,534
No, you tell me, is that right?

131
00:09:09,584 --> 00:09:10,380
Yes, sir, it's possible.

132
00:09:10,420 --> 00:09:11,520
What are you saying?

133
00:09:11,560 --> 00:09:13,740
You mean the English had contacted you?

134
00:09:13,780 --> 00:09:15,334
Honestly, why should I lie? The grave is

135
00:09:15,384 --> 00:09:16,450
this close!

136
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:17,684
To tell you the truth,

137
00:09:17,724 --> 00:09:18,884
yes.

138
00:09:19,384 --> 00:09:21,634
Don't talk nonsense, Mash Ghasem.

139
00:09:21,684 --> 00:09:25,864
Well, sir, if you think we talk nonsense, we'd better not talk at all.

140
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,590
Moment, moment, Colonel...

141
00:09:28,784 --> 00:09:30,074
Let him speak.

142
00:09:30,124 --> 00:09:32,054
We've nothing to say, sir.

143
00:09:32,104 --> 00:09:33,434
But Mash Ghasem,

144
00:09:33,484 --> 00:09:36,354
how could the English have contacted you?

145
00:09:36,404 --> 00:09:38,154
Today Sir got some notion in his head

146
00:09:38,204 --> 00:09:39,404
and said something.

147
00:09:39,444 --> 00:09:41,924
Didn't you swear yourself it was a lie?

148
00:09:41,964 --> 00:09:43,134
Honestly, sir, why should I lie?

149
00:09:43,184 --> 00:09:45,064
It was a lie, but not a lie.

150
00:09:45,364 --> 00:09:46,784
Mash Ghasem!

151
00:09:46,904 --> 00:09:49,774
Wasn't it me who taught you to confess?

152
00:09:49,824 --> 00:09:51,970
Didn't I tell you in the kitchen what to say?

153
00:09:52,020 --> 00:09:53,640
Now you're tricking us too?

154
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:56,000
True, you told us to say those things,

155
00:09:56,040 --> 00:09:57,920
but we didn't lie either.

156
00:09:57,960 --> 00:09:59,800
You mean the English really contacted you?

157
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:01,000
Yes, yes, yes, yes.

158
00:10:01,040 --> 00:10:03,000
Man, think a little!

159
00:10:03,040 --> 00:10:04,300
Why are you talking rubbish?

160
00:10:04,340 --> 00:10:05,280
Where did they contact you?

161
00:10:05,320 --> 00:10:06,700
When did they contact you?

162
00:10:06,740 --> 00:10:08,320
Why did they contact you?

163
00:10:08,360 --> 00:10:10,500
You won't let us get a word in.

164
00:10:10,540 --> 00:10:11,840
You're driving us crazy too.

165
00:10:11,880 --> 00:10:14,760
Go on, tell us, how did they contact you?

166
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:16,350
Oh, all right.

167
00:10:17,824 --> 00:10:19,120
It was just a few days ago.

168
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:22,075
One day we'd gone to the bakery.

169
00:10:23,724 --> 00:10:27,724
We saw an Englishman walk past the bakery two or three times.

170
00:10:28,404 --> 00:10:30,120
With those blasted cross eyes of his,

171
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:31,820
he kept giving us looks,

172
00:10:31,860 --> 00:10:34,180
he kept giving us looks,

173
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:37,005
like we were a fourteen-year-old girl.

174
00:10:37,644 --> 00:10:41,144
Then he came in and asked the baker something.

175
00:10:41,544 --> 00:10:45,544
When we came out, he kept right in step with us,

176
00:10:45,724 --> 00:10:48,054
as if, no offense, no offense,

177
00:10:48,544 --> 00:10:50,720
we were one of those flighty girls.

178
00:10:50,760 --> 00:10:55,080
When we reached the house, in a voice God spare you from hearing,

179
00:10:55,120 --> 00:10:58,940
a voice like a leopard's growl,

180
00:10:59,884 --> 00:11:07,900
in a language somewhere between Rashti, Turkish and Khorasani, he asked:

181
00:11:07,940 --> 00:11:10,760
"You're not from this neighborhood?"

182
00:11:11,564 --> 00:11:13,120
We didn't answer him.

183
00:11:13,160 --> 00:11:17,020
But to ourselves we said, "To hell with the English!"

184
00:11:17,060 --> 00:11:18,935
We ducked into the house

185
00:11:19,044 --> 00:11:20,660
and watched him through the door.

186
00:11:20,700 --> 00:11:23,520
We saw him go and look at every house.

187
00:11:23,560 --> 00:11:25,920
Then he knocked on the Indian's door and went in.

188
00:11:25,960 --> 00:11:27,020
Is that it?

189
00:11:27,060 --> 00:11:29,300
No, sir, that's just the beginning.

190
00:11:29,340 --> 00:11:31,100
Then we saw him again.

191
00:11:31,140 --> 00:11:34,200
He gave us a look that made our heart drop.

192
00:11:34,240 --> 00:11:37,420
Of course, of course the English contacted Mash Ghasem.

193
00:11:37,460 --> 00:11:39,660
Mash Ghasem, save the rest of the story for later.

194
00:11:39,700 --> 00:11:43,060
Now do you see what dangerous plans the English have for Sir?

195
00:11:43,100 --> 00:11:44,500
They've threatened us too.

196
00:11:44,540 --> 00:11:45,180
Of course.

197
00:11:45,220 --> 00:11:46,380
Certainly.

198
00:11:46,904 --> 00:11:49,880
We heard today the English want to contact Sir officially,

199
00:11:49,920 --> 00:11:51,660
to settle their differences.

200
00:11:51,700 --> 00:11:54,640
You mustn't fall for English tricks, sir.

201
00:11:54,680 --> 00:11:56,080
So now we're asking you a favor.

202
00:11:56,120 --> 00:11:57,580
When the English envoy comes to see Sir,

203
00:11:57,620 --> 00:12:00,820
Sir will surely want you to make sure he isn't outwitted.

204
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:02,160
Us, outwitted by the English?

205
00:12:02,200 --> 00:12:02,954
No.

206
00:12:02,994 --> 00:12:04,994
Us, outwitted by the English?

207
00:12:05,044 --> 00:12:09,814
We remember once in Ghias Abad, ten Englishmen jumped us.

208
00:12:09,864 --> 00:12:15,000
From dusk till dawn we kept swinging this shovel over our head.

209
00:12:15,932 --> 00:12:17,580
They all ran off.

210
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:19,980
Their leader said to one of his pals,

211
00:12:20,020 --> 00:12:23,540
"Let's go, this Mash Ghasem isn't the sort we thought."

212
00:12:23,580 --> 00:12:25,220
They hung their heads and left.

213
00:12:25,260 --> 00:12:27,720
And we shouted, "Hey, you cowards!

214
00:12:28,332 --> 00:12:30,020
We're not that sort!

215
00:12:30,060 --> 00:12:33,140
You'll take the water over our dead body!"

216
00:12:34,152 --> 00:12:35,460
Well, sir, it was a water feud.

217
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:39,460
Mash Ghasem, what's Ghias Abad's water got to do with the English?

218
00:12:39,500 --> 00:12:42,070
You're a long way from knowing the English!

219
00:12:42,120 --> 00:12:47,700
In all of Iran, there's no place the English hate like Ghias Abad!

220
00:12:48,312 --> 00:12:51,600
They wanted the water so Ghias Abad would be ruined and humbled.

221
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:52,440
Mash Ghasem...

222
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:55,660
Colonel! Mash Ghasem has a point.

223
00:12:55,700 --> 00:13:00,040
Cut off a town's water and, well, the people surrender.

224
00:13:00,080 --> 00:13:02,040
Napoleon took cities with that same trick.

225
00:13:02,080 --> 00:13:03,720
Hey, Mash Ghasem!

226
00:13:03,760 --> 00:13:05,380
Come, Sir wants you.

227
00:13:05,420 --> 00:13:06,180
Coming!

228
00:13:06,220 --> 00:13:08,220
We'll tell you the rest later.

229
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:10,395
Moment, Mash Ghasem, moment.

230
00:13:11,532 --> 00:13:13,300
What we ask of you is,

231
00:13:13,340 --> 00:13:16,502
when the English representative comes to see Sir,

232
00:13:16,552 --> 00:13:17,700
be careful

233
00:13:17,740 --> 00:13:19,700
that no harm comes to him.

234
00:13:19,740 --> 00:13:22,400
Or else the English will come and avenge his blood

235
00:13:22,440 --> 00:13:23,980
on you and Sir.

236
00:13:24,020 --> 00:13:26,000
After all, the English army is in Tehran.

237
00:13:26,040 --> 00:13:28,420
Their representative comes and talks with Sir.

238
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:30,400
If they reach agreement, all the better.

239
00:13:30,440 --> 00:13:32,000
If they don't,

240
00:13:32,040 --> 00:13:34,620
even if Sir angrily gives you some order,

241
00:13:34,660 --> 00:13:38,600
you must make sure the envoy leaves this house in one piece.

242
00:13:38,640 --> 00:13:40,660
You mean we should go against Sir's word?

243
00:13:40,700 --> 00:13:42,430
So he thinks we're English again?

244
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:45,362
You know the English better than anyone, Mash Ghasem.

245
00:13:45,412 --> 00:13:48,362
They're waiting for an excuse to attack Sir's house.

246
00:13:48,412 --> 00:13:50,050
Do you want to get killed?

247
00:13:50,100 --> 00:13:53,600
Sir, those English dogs couldn't kill us!

248
00:13:56,040 --> 00:13:58,000
Honestly, sir, we don't give a damn about these English.

249
00:13:58,040 --> 00:14:01,000
We're afraid they'll think that we, from Ghias Abad,

250
00:14:01,040 --> 00:14:03,000
God forbid, were scared of them.

251
00:14:03,040 --> 00:14:05,000
Good, very good.

252
00:14:05,040 --> 00:14:06,702
Look, my friend, I'd never want you

253
00:14:06,752 --> 00:14:08,302
or Sir to come to any harm or grief.

254
00:14:08,352 --> 00:14:09,202
I want to help you.

255
00:14:09,252 --> 00:14:10,942
We know, sir.

256
00:14:10,992 --> 00:14:13,040
Off you go, my friend.

257
00:14:20,040 --> 00:14:21,000
My head is spinning.

258
00:14:21,040 --> 00:14:24,010
I bet Mash Ghasem now fancies himself Talleyrand too.

259
00:14:24,050 --> 00:14:25,062
He finally agreed.

260
00:14:25,112 --> 00:14:27,702
For now he's very scared of the English.

261
00:14:27,752 --> 00:14:30,422
I doubt that even if Sir gives him a dangerous order,

262
00:14:30,472 --> 00:14:33,990
the Mash Ghasem I know would dare to carry it out.

263
00:14:34,040 --> 00:14:37,215
How about we move on to the second issue now?

264
00:14:38,092 --> 00:14:39,990
The Sardar says this corporal will only play the part

265
00:14:40,040 --> 00:14:42,952
in return for a pair of Isfahan rugs.

266
00:14:42,992 --> 00:14:45,472
I have two old rugs at home myself,

267
00:14:45,522 --> 00:14:46,510
but neither is from Isfahan.

268
00:14:46,550 --> 00:14:51,022
I think, Colonel... Brother has several Isfahan rugs. Maybe he could...

269
00:14:51,072 --> 00:14:53,800
Moment, moment, Colonel.

270
00:14:53,850 --> 00:14:58,760
You want to tell Sir that an English army colonel, Churchill's own envoy,

271
00:14:58,800 --> 00:15:01,202
must be paid a pair of rugs to forgive his sins?

272
00:15:01,252 --> 00:15:02,347
No.

273
00:15:02,592 --> 00:15:03,322
Well, couldn't we just...

274
00:15:03,362 --> 00:15:04,162
a pair.

275
00:15:04,202 --> 00:15:06,502
No, Colonel, you have to be generous.

276
00:15:06,552 --> 00:15:10,622
For your Brother's health, what's one rug?

277
00:15:10,672 --> 00:15:12,602
I'd give my life for Brother.

278
00:15:12,652 --> 00:15:17,142
I'm ready to give my life to ease Brother's troubles.

279
00:15:17,192 --> 00:15:19,742
But my rugs all come in pairs.

280
00:15:19,792 --> 00:15:20,522
It'll break the set.

281
00:15:20,572 --> 00:15:23,062
It's not worth one hair of your mustache.

282
00:15:23,112 --> 00:15:28,732
After long discussions, Dear Uncle agreed to join the peace talks.

283
00:15:28,932 --> 00:15:32,432
But the venue became a sticking point.

284
00:15:32,500 --> 00:15:36,350
Dear Uncle insisted the English representative come to his house,

285
00:15:36,400 --> 00:15:40,550
and Shazdeh held that Dear Uncle should go to English army headquarters.

286
00:15:40,592 --> 00:15:43,990
At last it was agreed that the peace talks would be held

287
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:47,700
on neutral ground, that is, our house.

288
00:16:01,420 --> 00:16:04,010
The Colonel sits here,

289
00:16:04,160 --> 00:16:06,360
the English representative here,

290
00:16:06,740 --> 00:16:09,240
and you, Asdollah, here.

291
00:16:09,500 --> 00:16:11,660
Moment, moment.

292
00:16:11,700 --> 00:16:15,380
I must sit at the English representative's right hand.

293
00:16:15,420 --> 00:16:19,680
No, as I said, you sit right here.

294
00:16:19,720 --> 00:16:22,640
I can't interpret from over there.

295
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:25,360
My place should be between you and the representative.

296
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:28,760
Isn't Sardar Maharat-Khan coming?

297
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:31,845
You yourself objected to his coming.

298
00:16:32,392 --> 00:16:33,487
Yes.

299
00:16:33,932 --> 00:16:37,780
In such important talks, it isn't wise

300
00:16:37,820 --> 00:16:39,200
for a stranger

301
00:16:39,240 --> 00:16:40,720
and an Indian at that,

302
00:16:40,760 --> 00:16:42,500
to be present.

303
00:16:42,540 --> 00:16:43,280
Yes, yes.

304
00:16:43,320 --> 00:16:44,760
You're absolutely right.

305
00:16:44,800 --> 00:16:45,880
Especially since these Indians

306
00:16:45,920 --> 00:16:47,700
speak English with a terrible

307
00:16:47,740 --> 00:16:48,280
accent.

308
00:16:48,320 --> 00:16:49,960
Your words might get twisted.

309
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:51,060
Very well.

310
00:16:51,100 --> 00:16:52,090
So you, Asdollah,

311
00:16:52,140 --> 00:16:53,760
right where you said,

312
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:54,600
sit.

313
00:16:54,640 --> 00:16:55,440
And Ghasem

314
00:16:55,480 --> 00:16:57,080
will stand behind me,

315
00:16:57,120 --> 00:16:58,900
two steps to the left.

316
00:16:58,940 --> 00:17:00,200
He'll stand there.

317
00:17:00,240 --> 00:17:01,660
I'm glad that mission

318
00:17:01,700 --> 00:17:02,920
you gave Mash Ghasem

319
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:03,820
is off.

320
00:17:03,860 --> 00:17:06,480
Guarding behind the curtain is quite unnecessary.

321
00:17:06,520 --> 00:17:08,260
God bless you, sir.

322
00:17:08,300 --> 00:17:10,700
We killed so many English in battle,

323
00:17:10,740 --> 00:17:12,540
it'll do us for seven generations.

324
00:17:12,580 --> 00:17:16,520
We don't want to dirty our hands with any more English blood.

325
00:17:16,560 --> 00:17:18,380
We remember we had a fellow townsman...

326
00:17:18,420 --> 00:17:21,640
For such an important mission,

327
00:17:21,680 --> 00:17:24,920
we need someone trustworthy.

328
00:17:25,772 --> 00:17:27,062
Come in.

329
00:17:28,692 --> 00:17:31,680
Pouri, as I ordered you,

330
00:17:31,720 --> 00:17:35,360
you'll stand behind the hallway door and keep watch.

331
00:17:35,400 --> 00:17:37,720
Keep your finger on the trigger.

332
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:40,120
The moment I give the order, you fire.

333
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:41,775
Yes, Uncle.

334
00:17:42,612 --> 00:17:45,080
But, Sir, in our negotiations

335
00:17:45,120 --> 00:17:46,980
we agreed the envoy would come unarmed.

336
00:17:47,020 --> 00:17:51,060
This is against all honor, morality, even the laws of war.

337
00:17:51,100 --> 00:17:53,840
I know the laws of war better than you.

338
00:17:53,880 --> 00:17:57,060
Never underestimate the enemy's malice.

339
00:17:58,072 --> 00:18:00,720
Go, Pouri, carry out your commander's order.

340
00:18:00,760 --> 00:18:02,245
Brother,

341
00:18:02,852 --> 00:18:04,640
this boy can't shoot at all.

342
00:18:04,680 --> 00:18:05,360
God forbid, what if...

343
00:18:05,400 --> 00:18:06,400
He can't?

344
00:18:06,440 --> 00:18:08,960
Then all this time he was a soldier,

345
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:09,940
what the hell was he doing?

346
00:18:09,980 --> 00:18:13,580
Well, he worked in the office.

347
00:18:13,620 --> 00:18:16,780
Well, of course he did some shooting too,

348
00:18:16,820 --> 00:18:18,660
but not with a Krupp rifle.

349
00:18:18,700 --> 00:18:19,860
Pouri,

350
00:18:20,844 --> 00:18:24,940
if you really aren't fit for this duty,

351
00:18:24,980 --> 00:18:27,600
before taking up your post,

352
00:18:27,640 --> 00:18:30,160
in all honesty,

353
00:18:30,200 --> 00:18:31,295
say so.

354
00:18:32,864 --> 00:18:34,674
As Napoleon said,

355
00:18:35,504 --> 00:18:37,260
admitting weakness

356
00:18:37,300 --> 00:18:39,840
is itself a kind of strength.

357
00:18:39,880 --> 00:18:41,235
Uncle,

358
00:18:41,804 --> 00:18:43,760
whatever you say.

359
00:18:43,800 --> 00:18:44,840
I'm ready

360
00:18:44,880 --> 00:18:47,620
to give my life for you.

361
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:50,420
Pouri, don't listen to anyone.

362
00:18:50,460 --> 00:18:51,500
Yes, Uncle.

363
00:18:51,540 --> 00:18:53,260
I only follow your orders.

364
00:18:53,300 --> 00:18:54,460
Go to your post.

365
00:18:54,500 --> 00:18:56,420
The commander's orders.

366
00:18:56,460 --> 00:18:57,260
Yes, sir.

367
00:18:57,300 --> 00:18:58,414
Moment.

368
00:18:58,464 --> 00:18:59,780
Shooting a practice rifle

369
00:18:59,820 --> 00:19:01,880
is different from shooting a Krupp rifle.

370
00:19:01,920 --> 00:19:03,100
If you'll allow me,

371
00:19:03,140 --> 00:19:05,020
I'll teach dear Pouri

372
00:19:05,060 --> 00:19:06,360
how to shoot this rifle.

373
00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:07,200
Let's go, my dear.

374
00:19:07,240 --> 00:19:08,740
Come, I'll teach you, come.

375
00:19:08,780 --> 00:19:10,640
Come, let me show you, come.

376
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:11,480
Give me that.

377
00:19:11,520 --> 00:19:12,235
Give me that.

378
00:19:12,275 --> 00:19:13,075
Oh!

379
00:19:14,604 --> 00:19:17,040
This thing is actually loaded!

380
00:19:17,080 --> 00:19:18,680
That's exactly what scares me.

381
00:19:18,720 --> 00:19:20,080
Uncle ordered it.

382
00:19:20,120 --> 00:19:20,920
Moment.

383
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:21,860
Moment.

384
00:19:21,900 --> 00:19:23,400
You're a sensible man.

385
00:19:23,440 --> 00:19:25,740
With great effort we managed

386
00:19:25,780 --> 00:19:28,374
to get the English envoy to come here and settle things,

387
00:19:28,424 --> 00:19:29,900
so your uncle gets better.

388
00:19:29,940 --> 00:19:32,440
Now suppose the talks fail,

389
00:19:32,480 --> 00:19:33,800
or there's some argument.

390
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:36,625
Should someone shoot the envoy in the belly?

391
00:19:36,684 --> 00:19:38,380
Don't you know they'd arrest you for murder

392
00:19:38,420 --> 00:19:39,480
and hang you?

393
00:19:39,520 --> 00:19:41,660
I won't really fire it.

394
00:19:41,700 --> 00:19:42,580
Uncle Asdollah...

395
00:19:42,620 --> 00:19:44,220
When there's a bullet in the gun,

396
00:19:44,260 --> 00:19:47,744
God forbid, one touch of your finger and it goes off.

397
00:19:47,784 --> 00:19:49,934
Doesn't... doesn't it have a safety?

398
00:19:49,984 --> 00:19:53,354
A relic like this? Where the hell would it keep a safety?

399
00:19:53,644 --> 00:19:55,454
Forgotten how the shock of a gunshot

400
00:19:55,504 --> 00:19:58,289
left you in such a state?

401
00:19:58,484 --> 00:20:00,374
That's if the barrel doesn't blow up on you.

402
00:20:00,424 --> 00:20:04,234
In just a few months, three or four of these blew up on people.

403
00:20:04,284 --> 00:20:06,574
Uncle Asdollah, I'm really scared.

404
00:20:06,624 --> 00:20:08,834
You're right to be. What are these?

405
00:20:08,884 --> 00:20:10,499
You call this a rifle?

406
00:20:11,264 --> 00:20:13,214
Uncle Asdollah, you took the bullets out!

407
00:20:13,264 --> 00:20:14,130
Keep quiet!

408
00:20:14,180 --> 00:20:15,140
Look,

409
00:20:15,180 --> 00:20:17,140
just stroll around here with your rifle.

410
00:20:17,180 --> 00:20:19,140
I promise you won't need to shoot.

411
00:20:19,180 --> 00:20:21,140
These rifles are no joke.

412
00:20:21,180 --> 00:20:23,140
Fifty times out of a hundred, they blow up.

413
00:20:23,180 --> 00:20:25,770
And right under the barrel, too.

414
00:20:26,204 --> 00:20:29,073
All the pellets hit the shooter below the belly.

415
00:20:29,123 --> 00:20:30,140
Want to be crippled at your age?

416
00:20:30,180 --> 00:20:31,925
Lose your manhood?

417
00:20:32,064 --> 00:20:34,050
And never make it to San Francisco?

418
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:36,200
Huh? Go on. Go.

419
00:20:37,344 --> 00:20:39,484
I don't follow you.

420
00:20:39,524 --> 00:20:42,044
I mean to say...

421
00:20:43,864 --> 00:20:45,349
how shall I put it?

422
00:20:50,940 --> 00:20:55,600
Well... this colonel who's about to call on you

423
00:20:55,700 --> 00:20:59,654
is one of the most distinguished men in the English army.

424
00:20:59,704 --> 00:21:02,284
Was it supposed to be otherwise?

425
00:21:02,444 --> 00:21:05,879
I don't negotiate with just any nobody.

426
00:21:11,184 --> 00:21:15,770
I mean, this colonel is one of Churchill's most trusted men.

427
00:21:15,820 --> 00:21:18,500
The mere fact that I agreed

428
00:21:18,540 --> 00:21:22,660
to negotiate with a colonel instead of a general,

429
00:21:22,700 --> 00:21:24,720
they should thank God for.

430
00:21:24,760 --> 00:21:25,560
Brother,

431
00:21:25,600 --> 00:21:28,640
you could say Churchill's right-hand man

432
00:21:28,680 --> 00:21:30,940
and commander of the English army

433
00:21:30,980 --> 00:21:32,220
is an Indian.

434
00:21:32,260 --> 00:21:33,600
An Indian?

435
00:21:33,640 --> 00:21:35,060
What did you say?

436
00:21:35,100 --> 00:21:36,500
An Indian?

437
00:21:37,404 --> 00:21:38,700
Ghasem, did you hear?

438
00:21:38,740 --> 00:21:39,560
Oh, good Lord!

439
00:21:39,600 --> 00:21:43,540
Sir, seems he's a countryman of our own Sardar Maharat-Khan.

440
00:21:43,580 --> 00:21:45,220
Oh, these Indians!

441
00:21:45,260 --> 00:21:47,620
Sir, they say this colonel is the Viceroy of India.

442
00:21:47,660 --> 00:21:48,460
Moment.

443
00:21:48,500 --> 00:21:49,660
Moment.

444
00:21:49,700 --> 00:21:50,540
Colonel,

445
00:21:50,580 --> 00:21:54,534
have you forgotten that Colonel Eshtiaq Khan has the title "Sir"?

446
00:21:54,584 --> 00:21:57,220
You must say Sir Eshtiaq Khan.

447
00:21:57,260 --> 00:22:03,380
Well, if he's the English government's plenipotentiary,

448
00:22:03,420 --> 00:22:05,460
what difference does it make to me?

449
00:22:05,500 --> 00:22:08,740
Right, what difference does it make?

450
00:22:14,500 --> 00:22:15,300
Mr. Shir-Ali.

451
00:22:15,340 --> 00:22:16,320
Mr. Shir-Ali?

452
00:22:16,360 --> 00:22:17,200
Did you want something?

453
00:22:17,240 --> 00:22:18,580
Gleetings, sil.

454
00:22:18,620 --> 00:22:19,640
Let him stay.

455
00:22:19,680 --> 00:22:25,260
I told him to come, so if we want tea or anything, he can help you.

456
00:22:26,224 --> 00:22:28,600
Make yourself at home. Naneh Belgheys isn't here.

457
00:22:28,640 --> 00:22:30,200
Help yourself to some tea.

458
00:22:30,240 --> 00:22:31,020
The samovar is on.

459
00:22:31,060 --> 00:22:33,260
Yes, sil, yes.

460
00:22:36,584 --> 00:22:39,160
So why hasn't this Indian Englishman come?

461
00:22:39,200 --> 00:22:41,530
Honestly, we're very worried.

462
00:22:43,564 --> 00:22:46,420
God rest his soul, we had a fellow townsman...

463
00:22:46,460 --> 00:22:47,260
Mash Ghasem!

464
00:22:47,300 --> 00:22:49,045
Yes, yes, all right.

465
00:23:00,260 --> 00:23:02,220
What is it, Shir-Ali?

466
00:23:02,260 --> 00:23:04,220
Sir, their guest has arrived.

467
00:23:04,260 --> 00:23:07,260
Shall I bling him in?

468
00:23:38,964 --> 00:23:40,384
Hello.

469
00:23:40,700 --> 00:23:42,234
Mr. Asadollah, how are you?

470
00:23:42,284 --> 00:23:44,150
Good morning, Sir Eshtiaq Khan.

471
00:23:44,200 --> 00:23:45,360
Come in please. Come in.

472
00:23:45,400 --> 00:23:47,920
Why do you call me sir? We had no such deal.

473
00:23:47,960 --> 00:23:50,350
No problem. This way the meeting is more formal.

474
00:23:50,400 --> 00:23:52,665
Sir... Sir Eshtiaq Khan.

475
00:23:53,400 --> 00:23:57,400
Good morning. I am glad to see you and have negotiation.

476
00:24:03,280 --> 00:24:05,285
What about his weapon?

477
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:08,730
He's clean, sir.

478
00:24:13,300 --> 00:24:15,110
Our greetings.

479
00:24:15,820 --> 00:24:17,435
Tell him to sit.

480
00:24:18,080 --> 00:24:19,990
please sit down sir

481
00:24:20,040 --> 00:24:21,210
please

482
00:24:21,260 --> 00:24:22,745
thank you

483
00:24:27,704 --> 00:24:29,160
You sit down too.

484
00:24:29,200 --> 00:24:30,220
Thank.

485
00:24:30,260 --> 00:24:33,480
Asdollah.

486
00:24:33,520 --> 00:24:34,680
Yes?

487
00:24:34,984 --> 00:24:38,364
Please translate everything I say word for word.

488
00:24:38,404 --> 00:24:39,204
Yes.

489
00:24:39,244 --> 00:24:40,114
Very well.

490
00:24:40,164 --> 00:24:45,680
Tell him my life, property and honor belong to my homeland.

491
00:24:45,720 --> 00:24:46,880
Right.

492
00:24:47,004 --> 00:24:50,460
If I must make concessions to the English,

493
00:24:51,284 --> 00:24:54,540
I'd a thousand times rather be killed

494
00:24:54,580 --> 00:24:58,620
and have my corpse fed to wolves and hyenas.

495
00:24:58,660 --> 00:25:00,080
Translate.

496
00:25:03,404 --> 00:25:05,344
Sir says that...

497
00:25:05,850 --> 00:25:12,100
I need to save my soul and my body for my, for my country.

498
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:16,700
If the British want to take advantage of me,

499
00:25:16,900 --> 00:25:21,570
I would rather die and have my body eaten by wolves

500
00:25:21,620 --> 00:25:22,780
And...

501
00:25:23,504 --> 00:25:24,894
Just a moment.

502
00:25:24,944 --> 00:25:26,254
Moment.

503
00:25:26,304 --> 00:25:28,850
I've forgotten how to say hyena in English.

504
00:25:28,900 --> 00:25:31,050
Hyena... what's hyena in English? Hyena.

505
00:25:31,100 --> 00:25:33,105
Some kind of scavenger, we reckon.

506
00:25:33,964 --> 00:25:36,724
Never mind the hyena.

507
00:25:37,644 --> 00:25:39,129
Tell him that...

508
00:25:39,384 --> 00:25:45,684
I'm aware of the enormous damage I did to the English army.

509
00:25:46,764 --> 00:25:49,580
In the battles of Kazerun,

510
00:25:49,620 --> 00:25:56,940
Mamasani and others, I may have wiped out thousands of English soldiers.

511
00:25:57,664 --> 00:26:02,660
I dealt their colonial ambitions the heaviest blows.

512
00:26:03,344 --> 00:26:07,644
But all of this was for my homeland.

513
00:26:07,684 --> 00:26:08,354
Yes.

514
00:26:08,404 --> 00:26:13,260
It was because the English had invaded my country.

515
00:26:13,864 --> 00:26:14,754
Translate.

516
00:26:14,804 --> 00:26:15,654
Yes.

517
00:26:15,704 --> 00:26:20,024
It was in the wars of Kazeroun and Mamasani.

518
00:26:20,064 --> 00:26:23,160
I killed thousands of English soldiers...

519
00:26:23,200 --> 00:26:30,350
And if I destroyed the English colonies, it was due to the invasion of my country.

520
00:26:30,444 --> 00:26:33,954
Why don't you take this gentleman to mental hospital?

521
00:26:34,004 --> 00:26:35,294
Moment.

522
00:26:35,900 --> 00:26:39,800
Colonel Sir Eshtiaq Khan says yes, yes,

523
00:26:39,840 --> 00:26:44,690
we know every detail of those wars,

524
00:26:44,740 --> 00:26:49,500
and for Sir, that is, you, as a patriot,

525
00:26:49,540 --> 00:26:51,150
we have great respect.

526
00:26:51,190 --> 00:26:51,990
But...

527
00:26:52,030 --> 00:26:52,940
Asdollah!

528
00:26:52,980 --> 00:26:56,200
The colonel only said two or three words.

529
00:26:56,240 --> 00:26:58,420
All that was in two or three words?

530
00:26:58,460 --> 00:27:00,340
You're not embellishing it, are you?

531
00:27:00,380 --> 00:27:02,740
Oh, come now, sir!

532
00:27:02,780 --> 00:27:05,474
Who knows English here, me or you?

533
00:27:05,524 --> 00:27:08,959
English is a language of brevity, my dear sir.

534
00:27:09,604 --> 00:27:12,160
Some words, if you want to put them into Persian,

535
00:27:12,200 --> 00:27:14,160
take half an hour of talking.

536
00:27:14,200 --> 00:27:17,340
Didn't you hear Churchill's speech the other night?

537
00:27:17,380 --> 00:27:19,440
He spoke for just ten minutes in the House of Commons,

538
00:27:19,480 --> 00:27:21,700
but the Persian, French and Arabic translations

539
00:27:21,740 --> 00:27:24,820
went on so long that everyone fell asleep.

540
00:27:24,860 --> 00:27:28,300
These English are capable of anything.

541
00:27:30,164 --> 00:27:33,220
Don't you remember, sir, when that sergeant came

542
00:27:33,260 --> 00:27:35,265
to beg you for mercy?

543
00:27:35,424 --> 00:27:37,689
He came in and said to us, "Fasalkh!"

544
00:27:38,704 --> 00:27:42,360
The interpreter took an hour to interpret it for us and Sir.

545
00:27:42,400 --> 00:27:44,200
And then said he'd forgotten half of it.

546
00:27:44,240 --> 00:27:46,280
Thought and thought and couldn't remember.

547
00:27:46,320 --> 00:27:47,740
Quiet, Mash Ghasem.

548
00:27:47,780 --> 00:27:50,414
So now you're an English expert too?

549
00:27:50,464 --> 00:27:52,940
You mean we, who fought the English forty years,

550
00:27:52,980 --> 00:27:54,060
don't know their language?

551
00:27:54,100 --> 00:27:56,200
Ghasem, shut up!

552
00:27:57,224 --> 00:27:58,940
Asdollah, hurry up and get to the point.

553
00:27:58,980 --> 00:28:02,154
Ask what proposal he's brought us.

554
00:28:02,204 --> 00:28:07,190
And tell him that fighting foreigners is in our blood.

555
00:28:07,240 --> 00:28:15,020
The late Great Sir in fact gave his life fighting foreigners.

556
00:28:15,060 --> 00:28:16,850
God rest his soul... Moment.

557
00:28:16,900 --> 00:28:17,810
Moment, moment.

558
00:28:17,850 --> 00:28:18,650
Yes.

559
00:28:18,690 --> 00:28:22,800
If you recall, Great Sir died in the cholera year.

560
00:28:23,604 --> 00:28:26,880
Don't talk nonsense, Asdollah. Translate what I say.

561
00:28:26,920 --> 00:28:31,044
Brother's right, Asdollah, stop embellishing.

562
00:28:31,084 --> 00:28:32,574
Yes, please.

563
00:28:32,624 --> 00:28:36,624
The rag you sent me is too small and not worth enough.

564
00:28:37,204 --> 00:28:44,720
Moment... Sir Eshtiaq Khan says his government knows your family's bravery,

565
00:28:44,760 --> 00:28:50,960
and today, if you formally pledge not to obstruct them,

566
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:55,120
he promises that after the war he'll favorably recommend your file to...

567
00:28:55,160 --> 00:28:55,960
Move it! Walk ahead of me!

568
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:57,200
Why are you dragging us?

569
00:28:57,240 --> 00:28:59,320
Stay where you are!

570
00:28:59,360 --> 00:29:00,360
Where do you think you're going?

571
00:29:00,400 --> 00:29:02,694
I'll settle this with Sir... You can't!

572
00:29:02,744 --> 00:29:04,340
Sir has a foreign guest.

573
00:29:04,380 --> 00:29:06,680
Let him have foreign guests! My business is urgent!

574
00:29:06,720 --> 00:29:07,960
Let go of me, I can walk.

575
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:11,000
Urgent business! I'll skin you alive, you shameless scoundrel!

576
00:29:11,040 --> 00:29:12,680
Can't you hear? He says you can't!

577
00:29:12,720 --> 00:29:13,940
Get in... let go of my hand!

578
00:29:13,980 --> 00:29:15,420
Shameless creep!

579
00:29:15,460 --> 00:29:19,350
Today I'm settling accounts with this bastard.

580
00:29:19,940 --> 00:29:24,020
What is this disgraceful scene?

581
00:29:24,060 --> 00:29:25,860
Can't you see we have a guest...

582
00:29:25,900 --> 00:29:28,000
But this shameless bastard was supposed

583
00:29:28,040 --> 00:29:31,880
to marry the poor girl and divorce her two months later.

584
00:29:31,920 --> 00:29:34,980
Not only didn't he divorce her, he got her pregnant!

585
00:29:35,020 --> 00:29:38,760
Now he wants to sell the Akbarabad estates and pocket the money.

586
00:29:38,800 --> 00:29:41,984
Brother, don't upset yourself.

587
00:29:42,044 --> 00:29:44,624
Hello there, fellow townsman.

588
00:29:46,224 --> 00:29:47,280
All well, God willing?

589
00:29:47,320 --> 00:29:50,880
Mash Karim from Ghias Abad came asking after you.

590
00:29:50,920 --> 00:29:53,340
I told him, he's forgotten his old friends.

591
00:29:53,380 --> 00:29:57,140
We're neighbors and we never see Mr. Ghias Abadi,

592
00:29:57,180 --> 00:29:58,280
let alone strangers.

593
00:29:58,320 --> 00:29:59,960
Oh, come now, my friend.

594
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:01,360
Hello, Mr. Aspiran.

595
00:30:01,400 --> 00:30:03,400
Greetings, Your Highness.

596
00:30:03,440 --> 00:30:05,380
Well! What's happened now?

597
00:30:05,464 --> 00:30:06,980
Sir, you must forgive me.

598
00:30:07,020 --> 00:30:09,380
This man's gone off his head.

599
00:30:09,420 --> 00:30:12,820
He tells me I have no right, with my own wife...

600
00:30:12,860 --> 00:30:15,420
Are you starting your nonsense again, you bastard?

601
00:30:15,460 --> 00:30:20,960
Well, well! Greetings, Eshtiaq Khan, how are you?

602
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:23,800
Long time no see! Just a few days ago

603
00:30:23,840 --> 00:30:27,280
I was asking Sardar Maharat-Khan about you.

604
00:30:29,840 --> 00:30:31,280
Dustali...

605
00:30:31,520 --> 00:30:36,240
You know Colonel Eshtiaq Khan?

606
00:30:36,308 --> 00:30:37,403
Huh?

607
00:30:41,100 --> 00:30:42,455
Huh? Huh?

608
00:30:44,560 --> 00:30:48,600
Since when is Corporal Eshtiaq Khan a colonel?

609
00:30:53,328 --> 00:30:55,918
Congratulations, Colonel.

610
00:30:56,628 --> 00:31:00,628
That time you went to Pas Qaleh with Sardar Maharat-Khan,

611
00:31:00,768 --> 00:31:02,708
you were still a corporal.

612
00:31:03,748 --> 00:31:05,103
What's wrong?

613
00:31:05,208 --> 00:31:06,560
Why are you staring like that?

614
00:31:06,600 --> 00:31:10,258
What I say? I also today come here for visit.

615
00:31:10,308 --> 00:31:12,560
I meet with Sir, I here today.

616
00:31:12,600 --> 00:31:19,200
Betrayal... betrayal... history repeats itself.

617
00:31:19,240 --> 00:31:21,120
Brother... Brother...

618
00:31:21,160 --> 00:31:29,560
Betrayal... betrayal, my brother... my brother... Lucien Bonaparte!

619
00:31:30,208 --> 00:31:32,668
Sir... Sir, how are you feeling?

620
00:31:33,520 --> 00:31:38,620
My brother-in-law... General Murat...

621
00:31:39,080 --> 00:31:41,380
Who has betrayed you?

622
00:31:42,080 --> 00:31:46,080
Shut up, General Marmont!

623
00:31:47,780 --> 00:31:50,950
Sir, we knew from the start it would end like this.

624
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:55,080
You shut up too, General Grouchy!

625
00:31:55,460 --> 00:31:59,580
Your record is the blackest of all.

626
00:31:59,840 --> 00:32:02,740
Brother...

627
00:32:03,160 --> 00:32:05,080
Get lost!

628
00:32:05,160 --> 00:32:09,168
I don't want to see any of your traitorous faces!

629
00:32:09,380 --> 00:32:13,380
Pouri, Shir-Ali, attack! Attack!

630
00:32:14,448 --> 00:32:17,340
Bastard!

631
00:32:17,380 --> 00:32:18,338
Why me like this?

632
00:32:18,388 --> 00:32:19,498
Me bastard?

633
00:32:19,548 --> 00:32:22,748
Me nothing did.

634
00:32:23,380 --> 00:32:27,330
Want to disgrace us in front of Sir? Get lost!

635
00:32:27,380 --> 00:32:29,340
A guest is a gift from God!

636
00:32:29,380 --> 00:32:31,340
Have you gone mad?

637
00:32:31,380 --> 00:32:32,278
A guest is a gift from God!

638
00:32:32,328 --> 00:32:35,998
Honestly, Sil told us to deal with his enemy.

639
00:32:36,048 --> 00:32:38,378
Where's your head, Shir-Ali dear?

640
00:32:38,748 --> 00:32:41,330
What enemy? This gentleman is Sir's friend.

641
00:32:41,380 --> 00:32:44,338
If friend, he my kind friend is,

642
00:32:44,388 --> 00:32:45,938
if darling, he my sweetheart,

643
00:32:45,988 --> 00:32:48,488
me say no, me come here, you say...

644
00:32:48,528 --> 00:32:50,728
Me no... me go...

645
00:32:50,888 --> 00:32:52,500
Poor thing's scared stiff.

646
00:32:52,540 --> 00:32:53,340
Let him through.

647
00:32:53,380 --> 00:32:54,340
Where is he?

648
00:32:54,380 --> 00:32:55,340
Go, go, go!

649
00:32:55,380 --> 00:32:56,340
Uncle Asdollah,

650
00:32:56,380 --> 00:32:57,865
let me...

651
00:32:59,300 --> 00:33:00,118
Shut up!

652
00:33:00,168 --> 00:33:02,078
Now this one's playing General Rommel on us.

653
00:33:02,128 --> 00:33:05,628
Shir-Ali, hold this jackass till I get back.

654
00:33:06,508 --> 00:33:10,008
I'll show you! I'll beat the hell out of you, bastard!

655
00:33:10,088 --> 00:33:12,028
Go on, sir, run for it!

656
00:33:15,380 --> 00:33:17,340
Shir-Ali, please,

657
00:33:17,380 --> 00:33:19,340
for God's sake, put me down.

658
00:33:19,380 --> 00:33:21,340
You idiot, if you'd laid a hand on this Indian,

659
00:33:21,380 --> 00:33:23,340
tomorrow the English would put two bullets in your empty head.

660
00:33:23,380 --> 00:33:25,330
But I wasn't really going to shoot him.

661
00:33:25,380 --> 00:33:28,130
I just wanted Uncle to hear me.

662
00:33:28,180 --> 00:33:29,880
Now you're the ones answering to him.

663
00:33:29,920 --> 00:33:31,990
Let him go now, Shir-Ali.

664
00:33:32,248 --> 00:33:33,540
You go out to the yard too.

665
00:33:33,580 --> 00:33:34,518
Go.

666
00:33:34,568 --> 00:33:35,820
And you, don't budge from here.

667
00:33:35,860 --> 00:33:37,820
Leave your uncle to me.

668
00:33:37,860 --> 00:33:39,020
The moment they set eyes on you,

669
00:33:39,060 --> 00:33:40,400
they'll raise hell again.

670
00:33:40,440 --> 00:33:41,240
Calm down.

671
00:33:41,280 --> 00:33:42,350
I'll show them!

672
00:33:42,400 --> 00:33:44,138
I made mincemeat of him.

673
00:33:44,188 --> 00:33:46,320
You've no idea how shamefully he fled.

674
00:33:46,360 --> 00:33:47,880
What a disgrace!

675
00:33:47,920 --> 00:33:48,860
He retreated.

676
00:33:48,900 --> 00:33:51,960
Nobody listens to me.

677
00:33:53,008 --> 00:33:58,660
Wretches, when I tell you that the tricks of this old fox,

678
00:33:58,700 --> 00:34:03,820
this crafty England, must not be ignored, you laugh at me.

679
00:34:04,048 --> 00:34:05,898
Let it rest for now.

680
00:34:05,948 --> 00:34:08,600
These gentlemen are a long way from understanding you.

681
00:34:08,640 --> 00:34:10,360
I swear by the oneness of God,

682
00:34:10,400 --> 00:34:12,925
Sir, why should I lie? The grave is this close!

683
00:34:13,720 --> 00:34:17,320
If we were Hitler, we'd make Sir our right-hand man

684
00:34:17,360 --> 00:34:19,700
to unmask these English for us.

685
00:34:19,740 --> 00:34:22,460
Never in our life have we seen such shamelessness,

686
00:34:22,500 --> 00:34:26,500
sending a sergeant to pass him off as the English chief!

687
00:34:27,200 --> 00:34:31,800
They did it on purpose.

688
00:34:33,628 --> 00:34:37,740
None of this surprises me at all.

689
00:34:38,440 --> 00:34:40,965
We've seen plenty of this.

690
00:34:42,588 --> 00:34:44,008
Napoleon, too,

691
00:34:44,908 --> 00:34:50,708
was deceived by the English after Waterloo.

692
00:34:51,200 --> 00:34:54,358
He abdicated. His fate...

693
00:34:54,408 --> 00:34:55,058
Yes.

694
00:34:55,108 --> 00:34:56,838
he put in their hands.

695
00:34:56,888 --> 00:34:58,598
They made him promises.

696
00:34:58,648 --> 00:35:00,133
Here you are.

697
00:35:02,288 --> 00:35:05,160
They made him promises.

698
00:35:05,200 --> 00:35:12,100
But in the end the poor man ended up on Saint Helena.

699
00:35:15,508 --> 00:35:19,380
My blood is no redder than Napoleon's.

700
00:35:19,500 --> 00:35:21,420
Calm down.

701
00:35:21,468 --> 00:35:24,560
They wanted to force me

702
00:35:25,468 --> 00:35:29,880
to negotiate with an Indian corporal,

703
00:35:30,708 --> 00:35:32,193
Here you are.

704
00:35:34,568 --> 00:35:41,600
to destroy the honor and dignity of me and my family.

705
00:35:41,640 --> 00:35:44,160
They wanted to humiliate me.

706
00:35:44,200 --> 00:35:45,260
All right, Brother,

707
00:35:45,300 --> 00:35:47,720
calm down, calm down, don't upset yourself.

708
00:35:47,760 --> 00:35:50,360
You'll be ill again! Calm down.

709
00:35:50,400 --> 00:35:52,470
How can I not be upset?

710
00:35:53,168 --> 00:35:54,698
How can I keep quiet?

711
00:35:54,748 --> 00:35:55,538
I beg you, calm down.

712
00:35:55,588 --> 00:35:59,260
In the face of this great conspiracy,

713
00:35:59,300 --> 00:36:02,008
how can I be calm?

714
00:36:02,048 --> 00:36:02,928
Please.

715
00:36:02,968 --> 00:36:08,420
They send an Indian corporal to negotiate with me,

716
00:36:08,460 --> 00:36:12,300
so tomorrow history will say:

717
00:36:13,068 --> 00:36:22,240
"A great fighter shamefully surrendered his sword to an Indian corporal."

718
00:36:22,280 --> 00:36:23,700
Here you are.

719
00:36:24,028 --> 00:36:26,980
Thank God their plot came to nothing.

720
00:36:27,030 --> 00:36:27,708
Don't be upset.

721
00:36:27,748 --> 00:36:31,540
In fact, you should be glad they couldn't carry out their plan.

722
00:36:31,580 --> 00:36:32,400
Don't you agree, Doctor?

723
00:36:32,440 --> 00:36:33,080
Yes, yes, yes.

724
00:36:33,120 --> 00:36:35,240
It was the hand of fate.

725
00:36:35,280 --> 00:36:36,080
Yes.

726
00:36:36,120 --> 00:36:39,200
If Dustali hadn't come along...

727
00:36:40,238 --> 00:36:42,040
Dustali, come here.

728
00:36:42,958 --> 00:36:44,053
Come.

729
00:36:48,618 --> 00:36:50,038
Closer.

730
00:36:50,858 --> 00:36:52,278
Closer.

731
00:36:57,398 --> 00:36:58,818
All these people

732
00:36:59,538 --> 00:37:00,750
were blind.

733
00:37:00,800 --> 00:37:02,230
Yes, yes.

734
00:37:02,278 --> 00:37:03,568
God

735
00:37:04,158 --> 00:37:06,000
moved your heart

736
00:37:06,040 --> 00:37:08,550
in this terrible maelstrom

737
00:37:08,600 --> 00:37:10,000
to come to my rescue,

738
00:37:10,040 --> 00:37:13,700
to come and foil this plot.

739
00:37:13,740 --> 00:37:15,840
From this day on, Dustali,

740
00:37:17,058 --> 00:37:20,680
you are my general.

741
00:37:21,498 --> 00:37:23,200
So now we're the bad guys?

742
00:37:23,240 --> 00:37:24,220
Never mind.

743
00:37:24,260 --> 00:37:28,260
As long as Sir calms down, let Dustali be the god of war.

744
00:37:28,538 --> 00:37:29,893
Dustali,

745
00:37:30,278 --> 00:37:31,698
my general.

746
00:37:32,478 --> 00:37:33,420
Now tell me,

747
00:37:33,460 --> 00:37:35,540
what quarrel do you have with

748
00:37:36,278 --> 00:37:39,060
Aspiran Ghias Abadi?

749
00:37:39,100 --> 00:37:41,260
You're the head of this family.

750
00:37:41,300 --> 00:37:44,600
Either settle things between me and this big oaf,

751
00:37:44,640 --> 00:37:46,860
or allow me, by legal means,

752
00:37:46,900 --> 00:37:51,320
to stop him attacking this family's lives, property and honor.

753
00:37:51,360 --> 00:37:55,320
First, the big oaf is whoever says so.

754
00:37:55,958 --> 00:37:59,790
Second, when did we attack your lives, property and honor?

755
00:37:59,830 --> 00:38:00,740
Sir, do you see?

756
00:38:00,780 --> 00:38:02,820
See how brazen he is?

757
00:38:02,860 --> 00:38:06,920
See what a lowlife we foolishly let into the family?

758
00:38:06,960 --> 00:38:09,680
To protect the family's reputation, we said

759
00:38:09,720 --> 00:38:12,920
this opium addict would come and marry Qamar,

760
00:38:12,960 --> 00:38:15,960
divorce her two months later and get two hundred tomans.

761
00:38:16,000 --> 00:38:19,440
We pitied that poor, sick, innocent girl

762
00:38:19,480 --> 00:38:22,700
whom some stranger had ruined.

763
00:38:22,740 --> 00:38:25,740
First, the sick innocent is you,

764
00:38:25,780 --> 00:38:27,460
not my wife.

765
00:38:27,738 --> 00:38:30,200
Second, the scoundrel is you.

766
00:38:30,240 --> 00:38:34,320
Third, the one who ruined her

767
00:38:34,360 --> 00:38:36,800
was no stranger at all.

768
00:38:36,840 --> 00:38:37,800
What?

769
00:38:37,840 --> 00:38:39,000
He wasn't.

770
00:38:39,538 --> 00:38:40,620
Not a stranger?

771
00:38:40,660 --> 00:38:41,480
No, he wasn't.

772
00:38:41,520 --> 00:38:42,620
You know him?

773
00:38:42,660 --> 00:38:43,740
Of course I know him.

774
00:38:43,780 --> 00:38:44,640
Then say it.

775
00:38:44,680 --> 00:38:47,440
Say who got this poor girl pregnant!

776
00:38:47,480 --> 00:38:48,260
Shall I?

777
00:38:48,300 --> 00:38:49,395
Say it!

778
00:38:50,517 --> 00:38:51,400
Yours truly!

779
00:38:51,440 --> 00:38:52,900
What?

780
00:38:52,940 --> 00:38:54,780
Yes, yours truly.

781
00:38:55,998 --> 00:38:58,020
Where'sh your head, brother?

782
00:38:58,838 --> 00:39:00,700
You're way behind, brother.

783
00:39:00,740 --> 00:39:03,500
Ever since Nayeb Teymoor-Khan and I

784
00:39:03,540 --> 00:39:06,060
came looking for your corpse,

785
00:39:06,100 --> 00:39:08,400
our heart's been set on Qamar.

786
00:39:09,898 --> 00:39:11,383
Ah, those were the days.

787
00:39:11,758 --> 00:39:12,800
What a night!

788
00:39:12,840 --> 00:39:14,760
What moonlight!

789
00:39:14,800 --> 00:39:15,760
I'm going to lose my mind!

790
00:39:15,800 --> 00:39:17,260
Shameless!

791
00:39:17,300 --> 00:39:19,000
The shameless liar!

792
00:39:19,040 --> 00:39:21,620
But when? Where? This creep didn't even know Qamar!

793
00:39:21,660 --> 00:39:22,460
Moment.

794
00:39:22,500 --> 00:39:24,600
Moment, Mr. Dustali Khan.

795
00:39:24,640 --> 00:39:27,500
The officer himself says the child is his.

796
00:39:27,540 --> 00:39:29,260
And you say it's some stranger's?

797
00:39:29,300 --> 00:39:30,680
Have some shame.

798
00:39:30,720 --> 00:39:32,880
Either you accept what the officer says,

799
00:39:32,920 --> 00:39:34,880
or tell us who the father is.

800
00:39:34,920 --> 00:39:36,040
Besides,

801
00:39:36,080 --> 00:39:38,580
people don't go to San Francisco in front of you and me.

802
00:39:38,620 --> 00:39:40,620
San Francisco is a city

803
00:39:40,660 --> 00:39:42,040
that only lets two people in.

804
00:39:42,080 --> 00:39:45,280
Shut up, Asdollah! Wasn't it right in front of you that

805
00:39:45,320 --> 00:39:47,060
this lowlife

806
00:39:47,100 --> 00:39:48,880
said that filthy thing of his,

807
00:39:48,920 --> 00:39:49,720
that...

808
00:39:49,760 --> 00:39:52,440
that damned organ of his,

809
00:39:52,480 --> 00:39:55,000
got shot in the war and is no good anymore?

810
00:39:55,040 --> 00:39:56,440
Wasn't it him?

811
00:39:56,480 --> 00:39:58,400
He never told me anything about his organ.

812
00:39:58,440 --> 00:39:59,990
He didn't? No, sir.

813
00:40:00,758 --> 00:40:03,940
Actually, he's right about that.

814
00:40:03,980 --> 00:40:05,790
I said it myself.

815
00:40:06,238 --> 00:40:09,283
We're old hands at this, see.

816
00:40:09,340 --> 00:40:12,820
We've caught a thousand criminals by now.

817
00:40:12,860 --> 00:40:15,040
We said we'd been shot

818
00:40:15,080 --> 00:40:20,898
so they wouldn't nab us and hand us to the courts and the polishe.

819
00:40:23,200 --> 00:40:26,648
Bravo! Long live Mr. Ghias Abadi!

820
00:40:26,698 --> 00:40:29,613
I'd never have guessed you were so clever.

821
00:40:31,258 --> 00:40:32,978
Oh, good Lord!

822
00:40:33,158 --> 00:40:35,163
What brains, what talent!

823
00:40:35,298 --> 00:40:40,160
Didn't we say no one beats Ghias Abad men for manhood?

824
00:40:40,200 --> 00:40:42,510
Long live Ghias Abad!

825
00:40:42,550 --> 00:40:44,388
Well done! Bravo!

826
00:40:44,438 --> 00:40:46,148
You've made our day.

827
00:40:46,198 --> 00:40:48,928
From today, you're an honorary citizen of San Francisco.

828
00:40:48,978 --> 00:40:53,980
We're your humble servant, Your Highness. You're too kind.

829
00:40:56,218 --> 00:40:58,130
What? Why are you looking at me like that?

830
00:40:58,180 --> 00:41:02,480
I love my wife, and my wife loves me.

831
00:41:02,680 --> 00:41:06,480
We have a darling child, and a second on the way.

832
00:41:07,480 --> 00:41:11,480
And to Mr. Dushtali Khan, that's dishonorable.

833
00:41:11,680 --> 00:41:12,630
Why?

834
00:41:12,680 --> 00:41:16,130
Because we had a child with our lawful wife.

835
00:41:16,180 --> 00:41:18,930
While he goes sneaking into married women's houses.

836
00:41:18,980 --> 00:41:19,640
Me?

837
00:41:19,680 --> 00:41:23,880
I go to married women's houses? Me, you shameless creep?

838
00:41:23,920 --> 00:41:27,360
Yes. On Wednesday, who was it

839
00:41:27,400 --> 00:41:31,980
that sneaked out of Shir-Ali the butcher's house?

840
00:41:32,020 --> 00:41:36,020
Dustali? Is it true?

841
00:41:36,060 --> 00:41:36,980
Shut up, Asdollah!

842
00:41:37,020 --> 00:41:38,640
Honesty is the best policy.

843
00:41:38,680 --> 00:41:42,320
So you finally went to San Francisco with Tahereh? All the way into town?

844
00:41:42,360 --> 00:41:43,840
I'll make you pay, I warn you!

845
00:41:43,880 --> 00:41:46,200
Stop picking on me or you'll be sorry!

846
00:41:46,240 --> 00:41:48,310
Moment, moment, my friend.

847
00:41:49,098 --> 00:41:49,968
Shir-Ali's wife wasn't enough,

848
00:41:50,018 --> 00:41:51,200
now you want to come after me?

849
00:41:51,240 --> 00:41:55,000
What's he been drinking to turn into such a devil?

850
00:41:55,240 --> 00:41:57,888
I'll smash your brains in, I warn you!

851
00:41:57,938 --> 00:41:59,293
Come again?

852
00:42:01,158 --> 00:42:02,448
Shir-Ali.

853
00:42:02,878 --> 00:42:04,190
Mr. Shir-Ali.

854
00:42:04,240 --> 00:42:05,725
Dear friend.

855
00:42:06,240 --> 00:42:08,200
Hey, Shir-Ali!

856
00:42:08,240 --> 00:42:10,200
Yes, sir, any oldels?

857
00:42:10,240 --> 00:42:11,200
But you've got some already.

858
00:42:11,240 --> 00:42:13,200
I wanted to ask you for some more tea.

859
00:42:13,240 --> 00:42:14,660
Here you are.

860
00:42:17,478 --> 00:42:19,223
Gleetings.

861
00:42:34,658 --> 00:42:35,753
No.

862
00:42:37,558 --> 00:42:39,500
Yes, as I was saying,

863
00:42:39,540 --> 00:42:42,100
people who care about their honor

864
00:42:42,140 --> 00:42:43,200
get upset,

865
00:42:43,240 --> 00:42:45,560
whether they're high-ranking or low,

866
00:42:45,600 --> 00:42:48,840
rich or poor, it makes no difference.

867
00:42:48,954 --> 00:42:50,760
Take Mr. Shir-Ali here, for example.

868
00:42:50,800 --> 00:42:53,130
Mr. Shir-Ali, God forbid,

869
00:42:53,494 --> 00:42:54,340
God forbid.

870
00:42:54,380 --> 00:42:56,980
Suppose you had a friend,

871
00:42:57,020 --> 00:42:59,080
a pal,

872
00:42:59,120 --> 00:43:00,800
and one day, when you went to work,

873
00:43:00,840 --> 00:43:02,975
you found out that pal of yours

874
00:43:03,354 --> 00:43:05,684
had gone to your house while you were out.

875
00:43:05,814 --> 00:43:07,200
How would you feel?

876
00:43:07,240 --> 00:43:09,520
Please, sil, don't say such things.

877
00:43:09,560 --> 00:43:11,600
When you say things like that,

878
00:43:11,640 --> 00:43:15,060
God folbid, I get into a state.

879
00:43:15,100 --> 00:43:15,760
I want to...

880
00:43:15,800 --> 00:43:17,740
I want to... everything...

881
00:43:18,354 --> 00:43:19,080
I'm burned!

882
00:43:19,120 --> 00:43:20,184
What happened?

883
00:43:20,234 --> 00:43:21,900
What have you done, Shir-Ali?

884
00:43:21,950 --> 00:43:23,300
I'm burned! Ow, ow, ow!

885
00:43:23,350 --> 00:43:24,445
What?

886
00:43:25,214 --> 00:43:26,250
Ow, ow, I'm burned!

887
00:43:26,300 --> 00:43:27,770
What? Who's burned?

888
00:43:27,820 --> 00:43:30,740
Instead of getting some medicine, you just keep screaming!

889
00:43:30,780 --> 00:43:32,420
Whoever burned you, what'll you do to him?

890
00:43:32,460 --> 00:43:33,500
He didn't do it on purpose.

891
00:43:33,540 --> 00:43:35,500
Shir-Ali spilled tea on your dad's head.

892
00:43:35,540 --> 00:43:37,040
Go get a needle and pop the blister.

893
00:43:37,080 --> 00:43:38,560
Then you tell me what to do!

894
00:43:38,600 --> 00:43:39,800
Nothing. Out you go.

895
00:43:39,840 --> 00:43:41,340
As long as you're not screaming,

896
00:43:41,380 --> 00:43:43,214
patients get better by themselves.

897
00:43:43,254 --> 00:43:44,054
I'm burned!

898
00:43:44,094 --> 00:43:45,709
Calm down.

899
00:43:46,314 --> 00:43:47,520
I'm burned!

900
00:43:47,560 --> 00:43:48,360
Let me see.

901
00:43:48,400 --> 00:43:49,204
I'm burned, I'm on fire!

902
00:43:49,244 --> 00:43:50,044
Oh, come on.

903
00:43:50,084 --> 00:43:51,640
Whose fault is it?

904
00:43:51,800 --> 00:43:54,510
This... this lowlife!

905
00:43:54,550 --> 00:43:58,500
He's a thief, a predator, a swindler, a murderer!

906
00:43:58,714 --> 00:44:00,884
So you're blinding the Colonel now, are you?

907
00:44:00,934 --> 00:44:04,944
Did I have anything to do with it, sir?

908
00:44:04,994 --> 00:44:07,304
I'll show you who you're dealing with!

909
00:44:07,354 --> 00:44:09,384
Burning the Colonel's face, are you?

910
00:44:09,434 --> 00:44:11,744
I'm waiting for you to show me.

911
00:44:11,794 --> 00:44:13,224
This is ridiculous.

912
00:44:13,274 --> 00:44:14,404
Do something!

913
00:44:14,454 --> 00:44:17,874
You're upset that my wife got pregnant.

914
00:44:17,994 --> 00:44:21,590
What's that got to do with the Colonel's face getting burned?

915
00:44:21,640 --> 00:44:24,160
Am I the tap on the shamovar?

916
00:44:24,200 --> 00:44:25,760
The officer's right.

917
00:44:25,800 --> 00:44:27,440
Is he the samovar tap, sir?

918
00:44:27,480 --> 00:44:29,320
You shut up! Shut up, shut up, shut up!

919
00:44:29,360 --> 00:44:32,920
Asdollah, is this a time for jokes? Come help!

920
00:44:32,960 --> 00:44:34,000
Sir isn't well.

921
00:44:34,040 --> 00:44:35,140
Shir-Ali, kindly wait outside.

922
00:44:35,180 --> 00:44:36,220
I need a word with you.

923
00:44:36,260 --> 00:44:37,240
I'll call you later.

924
00:44:37,280 --> 00:44:39,360
What did you give him?

925
00:44:40,994 --> 00:44:45,694
These heartless people have killed Sir!

926
00:44:49,374 --> 00:44:52,094
Ow, ow, I'm burned, I'm burned, I'm on fire!

927
00:44:52,214 --> 00:44:56,064
I'm burned, burned, burned, burned!

928
00:44:56,114 --> 00:44:59,070
I'm burned, I'm on fire! Who cares how I am?

929
00:44:59,120 --> 00:45:01,510
Who cares that poor me is burned?

930
00:45:01,560 --> 00:45:03,630
What are you saying? Come now, Colonel,

931
00:45:03,680 --> 00:45:05,070
everyone's worried about you.

932
00:45:05,120 --> 00:45:06,750
But your Brother isn't well.

933
00:45:06,800 --> 00:45:08,310
Let him catch his breath.

934
00:45:08,360 --> 00:45:10,670
You think I'm well?

935
00:45:10,720 --> 00:45:13,120
I'm burned, I'm in flames!

936
00:45:13,200 --> 00:45:14,070
I'm roasted!

937
00:45:14,120 --> 00:45:16,255
He'll catch his breath in a moment.

938
00:45:17,554 --> 00:45:19,700
This one's dying, and he thinks only of himself.

939
00:45:19,740 --> 00:45:20,920
Do something!

940
00:45:20,960 --> 00:45:22,460
Do something!

941
00:45:22,500 --> 00:45:23,300
Oh, oh, oh!

942
00:45:23,340 --> 00:45:24,070
Breathe out.

943
00:45:24,120 --> 00:45:25,460
I'm roasted!

944
00:45:25,500 --> 00:45:26,200
I'm on fire!

945
00:45:26,240 --> 00:45:27,040
Let me see.

946
00:45:27,080 --> 00:45:28,085
I'm burned!

947
00:45:28,474 --> 00:45:29,820
Let me see.

948
00:45:30,254 --> 00:45:31,364
I'm on fire!

949
00:45:31,414 --> 00:45:34,524
Take your hand away so I can see what's happened.

950
00:45:38,654 --> 00:45:39,404
Oh, oh!

951
00:45:39,454 --> 00:45:42,280
The skin on his face is roasted through.

952
00:45:42,320 --> 00:45:44,260
It's a bad burn.

953
00:45:44,750 --> 00:45:47,450
Ah, I'm burned, I'm burned!

954
00:45:47,500 --> 00:45:49,444
I'm roasted, I'm roasted! Help me!

955
00:45:49,494 --> 00:45:51,164
Oh, good Lord!

956
00:45:51,214 --> 00:45:55,094
The Colonel's face looks like something fried...

957
00:45:56,034 --> 00:45:58,004
The poor man's badly burned.

958
00:45:58,054 --> 00:46:00,644
Mr. Asdollah Mirza, we know the cure for this.

959
00:46:00,694 --> 00:46:03,344
We had a fellow townsman, and this townsman...

960
00:46:03,394 --> 00:46:04,184
Mash Ghasem.

961
00:46:04,224 --> 00:46:04,560
Yes.

962
00:46:04,600 --> 00:46:06,600
Going to talk rubbish again, man?

963
00:46:06,650 --> 00:46:08,800
We talk rubbish? We talk rubbish?

964
00:46:08,850 --> 00:46:13,200
Honestly, sir, why should I lie? The grave is this close!

965
00:46:13,250 --> 00:46:17,010
When it comes to burns, we're as good as any doctor.

966
00:46:18,100 --> 00:46:19,520
Oh, oh!

967
00:46:20,400 --> 00:46:23,300
Yes, there's only one cure for this.

968
00:46:23,500 --> 00:46:25,650
What cure? There's no cure for this burn.

969
00:46:25,700 --> 00:46:28,100
What should I do?

970
00:46:28,150 --> 00:46:29,765
Let me see.

971
00:46:30,154 --> 00:46:31,650
I'm on fire!

972
00:46:31,700 --> 00:46:34,410
Honestly, sir, no offense, no offense, God forbid,

973
00:46:34,450 --> 00:46:39,000
pardon my language, the only cure is a little boy's pee rubbed on it.

974
00:46:40,094 --> 00:46:41,330
Oh, good Lord!

975
00:46:41,380 --> 00:46:45,580
Now where do we find a little boy to, on the Colonel's face...

976
00:46:45,620 --> 00:46:46,800
Shut your trap!

977
00:46:46,840 --> 00:46:50,940
So you want to rub any filth you can find on my poor face?

978
00:46:51,280 --> 00:46:52,080
I'm burned!

979
00:46:52,120 --> 00:46:56,720
Instead of this nonsense, Mash Ghasem, go put some oil on the Colonel's face.

980
00:46:56,760 --> 00:46:59,410
Almond oil, castor oil, some kind of oil.

981
00:46:59,450 --> 00:47:03,620
Ow, ow, I'm on fire! I'm on fire!

982
00:47:03,674 --> 00:47:05,320
To hell with the Colonel's face!

983
00:47:05,360 --> 00:47:07,440
We must stay here and do something for Sir.

984
00:47:07,480 --> 00:47:09,720
Don't worry about Sir, his pulse has settled.

985
00:47:09,760 --> 00:47:10,640
He'll be fine. Go.

986
00:47:10,680 --> 00:47:11,480
Yes, sir.

987
00:47:11,520 --> 00:47:12,500
Oil!

988
00:47:15,074 --> 00:47:16,720
Sir, we've no almond or castor oil.

989
00:47:16,760 --> 00:47:18,800
Shall we rub some cooking butter on him in the kitchen?

990
00:47:18,840 --> 00:47:21,820
Rub on whatever there is! Go on, take him!

991
00:47:22,474 --> 00:47:23,724
I'm burned!

992
00:47:23,774 --> 00:47:25,100
This way, Colonel.

993
00:47:25,150 --> 00:47:26,424
I'm on fire!

994
00:47:26,474 --> 00:47:29,014
Oh, oh, oh!

995
00:47:34,374 --> 00:47:35,050
Look at the Colonel.

996
00:47:35,100 --> 00:47:37,060
Just like a child, look at him.

997
00:47:37,100 --> 00:47:40,304
But nothing works like that cure we told you about, mind.

998
00:47:40,354 --> 00:47:41,514
Take him.

999
00:47:41,974 --> 00:47:43,050
You'd better go next door too.

1000
00:47:43,100 --> 00:47:45,060
Sir needs a little rest.

1001
00:47:45,100 --> 00:47:47,060
Please.

1002
00:47:47,100 --> 00:47:50,364
I swore that until I settle things with this creep,

1003
00:47:50,414 --> 00:47:52,434
I won't set foot outside this house.

1004
00:47:52,484 --> 00:47:54,100
I'm staying till Sir recovers.

1005
00:47:54,150 --> 00:47:57,130
I'll have the officer throw you out by the scruff of the neck.

1006
00:48:21,100 --> 00:48:23,120
I'm not budging from here.

1007
00:48:23,160 --> 00:48:24,600
Not budging, eh?

1008
00:48:24,640 --> 00:48:25,640
No.

1009
00:48:25,680 --> 00:48:26,580
Moment.

1010
00:48:26,620 --> 00:48:28,040
Oh, get lost.

1011
00:48:28,414 --> 00:48:29,639
Moment.

1012
00:48:30,334 --> 00:48:31,700
Hey, Shir-Ali!

1013
00:48:31,740 --> 00:48:33,940
Don't worry, the corporal's gone.

1014
00:48:33,980 --> 00:48:35,200
We threw him out.

1015
00:48:35,240 --> 00:48:36,854
You threw him out?

1016
00:48:36,894 --> 00:48:38,054
Yes.

1017
00:48:38,514 --> 00:48:41,300
Good, you did well to throw him out.

1018
00:48:41,340 --> 00:48:42,600
Forget it, sir.

1019
00:48:42,640 --> 00:48:44,560
I kicked him out of the house myself.

1020
00:48:44,600 --> 00:48:45,630
I...

1021
00:48:49,774 --> 00:48:51,129
My time

1022
00:48:51,814 --> 00:48:53,234
has come.

1023
00:48:54,294 --> 00:48:57,520
Don't you bow to disgrace.

1024
00:48:59,244 --> 00:49:02,720
We fought side by side.

1025
00:49:04,624 --> 00:49:06,304
Shoulder to shoulder.

1026
00:49:06,780 --> 00:49:09,720
We stood back to back.

1027
00:49:11,484 --> 00:49:15,720
And now we'll be taken prisoner together.

1028
00:49:19,444 --> 00:49:21,720
Do you see, Dustali?

1029
00:49:22,684 --> 00:49:27,880
Do you see how a great general lives?

1030
00:49:29,644 --> 00:49:31,340
You too, along with me,

1031
00:49:31,380 --> 00:49:33,140
will be taken prisoner.

1032
00:49:35,064 --> 00:49:36,159
But

1033
00:49:37,084 --> 00:49:38,960
with honor.

1034
00:49:39,000 --> 00:49:41,420
I'm already a prisoner, Sir,

1035
00:49:41,460 --> 00:49:43,400
a prisoner of this devil incarnate.

1036
00:49:43,440 --> 00:49:47,440
I came here so you'd settle things between me and this creep.

1037
00:49:50,384 --> 00:49:54,440
The officer's been taken prisoner with us too.

1038
00:49:59,184 --> 00:50:01,660
My dear officer.

1039
00:50:01,700 --> 00:50:02,860
No, sir.

1040
00:50:03,080 --> 00:50:04,640
He's gone off his head.

1041
00:50:04,680 --> 00:50:07,790
You're the one off your head, you shameless creep!

1042
00:50:08,064 --> 00:50:10,394
How brazen you two are!

1043
00:50:12,184 --> 00:50:12,984
You'd hit me?

1044
00:50:13,024 --> 00:50:13,654
You bet I would!

1045
00:50:13,704 --> 00:50:15,574
Has it come to this?

1046
00:50:15,624 --> 00:50:17,564
Traitor!

1047
00:50:17,700 --> 00:50:19,575
Out, both of you!

1048
00:50:48,000 --> 00:50:50,070
Sir, are you leaving?

1049
00:50:53,500 --> 00:50:57,400
We're going... to pack our bags.

1050
00:51:03,344 --> 00:51:05,609
Let me help you.

1051
00:51:06,024 --> 00:51:07,249
No, no.

1052
00:51:21,504 --> 00:51:23,214
Want to swallow up my estates?

1053
00:51:23,264 --> 00:51:26,764
Your estates?

1054
00:51:43,024 --> 00:51:45,420
Ghasem, is that you?

1055
00:51:45,460 --> 00:51:49,520
Yes, sir, it's us. Are you going home, sir?

1056
00:51:49,560 --> 00:51:55,420
Yes, we're going to get ready to leave.

1057
00:51:55,460 --> 00:51:59,660
Captivity is our heritage.

1058
00:52:00,264 --> 00:52:03,340
But captivity with honor.

1059
00:52:03,984 --> 00:52:04,754
Ghasem.

1060
00:52:04,804 --> 00:52:05,964
Yes.

1061
00:52:52,784 --> 00:52:53,944
Hello.

1062
00:52:54,004 --> 00:52:56,464
Uncle Asdollah, Farrokh Lagha's here.

1063
00:52:58,544 --> 00:53:00,660
That old crow's turned up again.

1064
00:53:00,700 --> 00:53:02,840
Well, well, what a gathering!

1065
00:53:02,880 --> 00:53:05,120
What a surprise to see you here!

1066
00:53:05,160 --> 00:53:08,220
I went to pay my condolences at Monir Khanom's house,

1067
00:53:08,260 --> 00:53:10,740
and on the way back I thought I'd drop in on you.

1068
00:53:10,780 --> 00:53:12,180
Which Monir Khanom?

1069
00:53:12,220 --> 00:53:15,480
Monir Khanom, you know, Etemad-ol-Mamalek's daughter.

1070
00:53:15,520 --> 00:53:16,480
Ah, yes, yes.

1071
00:53:16,520 --> 00:53:18,560
Poor thing, she's had such bad luck lately.

1072
00:53:18,600 --> 00:53:21,160
Her poor husband came home from the office at noon,

1073
00:53:21,200 --> 00:53:25,014
went to the pool to wash, had a stroke and fell into the water.

1074
00:53:25,064 --> 00:53:28,580
He wasn't even old. By the time they got a doctor, he was gone.

1075
00:53:28,620 --> 00:53:31,220
The memorial is this afternoon.

1076
00:53:31,260 --> 00:53:34,500
Everyone says grief over his brother-in-law killed him.

1077
00:53:35,300 --> 00:53:37,960
What happened to his brother-in-law?

1078
00:53:38,000 --> 00:53:42,640
A few days ago the English arrested him with some others and exiled them.

1079
00:53:42,680 --> 00:53:46,245
They say they sent them all to Arak in chains.

1080
00:53:46,744 --> 00:53:48,890
The English? Why?

1081
00:53:48,940 --> 00:53:52,740
Bless them, this family is so big

1082
00:53:52,780 --> 00:53:55,080
we have to go to someone's memorial every day.

1083
00:53:55,120 --> 00:53:57,580
Asdollah, wait a moment.

1084
00:53:58,740 --> 00:54:04,480
Madam, you said the English arrested some people and sent them to Arak?

1085
00:54:04,520 --> 00:54:06,280
Yes, sir.

1086
00:54:06,320 --> 00:54:09,300
Poor Etemad's brother-in-law was one of them.

1087
00:54:09,340 --> 00:54:12,620
The poor man had nothing to do with any of it.

1088
00:54:13,020 --> 00:54:17,440
The English... the English have made their move.

1089
00:54:18,240 --> 00:54:22,440
Pouri, Ghasem, let's go home.

1090
00:54:22,780 --> 00:54:24,780
Let's go.

1091
00:54:32,580 --> 00:54:34,540
Why is Sir so upset?

1092
00:54:34,580 --> 00:54:39,380
Sir didn't even know Monir Khanom's husband or his brother-in-law.

1093
00:54:40,780 --> 00:54:43,740
Sir is upset over poor Mansur-ol-Saltaneh,

1094
00:54:43,780 --> 00:54:45,340
Dustali's uncle.

1095
00:54:45,380 --> 00:54:47,840
Why, what's wrong with Dustali's uncle?

1096
00:54:48,780 --> 00:54:49,740
Strange.

1097
00:54:49,780 --> 00:54:52,340
Strange that you haven't heard, madam.

1098
00:54:52,380 --> 00:54:53,670
Poor man.

1099
00:54:54,380 --> 00:54:55,840
God rest his soul.

1100
00:54:55,880 --> 00:54:57,340
He suffered so much in his illness.

1101
00:54:57,380 --> 00:55:00,425
Heaven help me, how did I not hear?

1102
00:55:00,880 --> 00:55:03,340
Your late mother was so close to them,

1103
00:55:03,380 --> 00:55:05,840
I thought you'd be the one laying out the body.

1104
00:55:05,880 --> 00:55:07,840
You're right, how awful.

1105
00:55:07,880 --> 00:55:11,120
I'd better go see Aziz-Saltaneh while it's early.

1106
00:55:11,880 --> 00:55:13,840
You really tricked her!

1107
00:55:13,880 --> 00:55:15,840
The crow's flown back to her graveyard.

1108
00:55:15,880 --> 00:55:18,340
First that idiot Dustali ruined our plan,

1109
00:55:18,380 --> 00:55:20,340
and now this woman.

1110
00:55:20,380 --> 00:55:22,340
She really is a bird of ill omen.

1111
00:55:22,380 --> 00:55:24,340
Dustali wasn't so much to blame.

1112
00:55:24,380 --> 00:55:26,340
Believe me, deep down, Sir,

1113
00:55:26,380 --> 00:55:28,330
without even realizing it,

1114
00:55:28,380 --> 00:55:30,340
longs to be taken prisoner.

1115
00:55:30,380 --> 00:55:32,340
I'm sure he's waiting for a fate

1116
00:55:32,380 --> 00:55:35,425
like Napoleon's.

1117
00:55:35,820 --> 00:55:37,090
Even if the Indian corporal

1118
00:55:37,140 --> 00:55:39,330
had granted him every concession,

1119
00:55:39,380 --> 00:55:40,340
in the end he'd still

1120
00:55:40,380 --> 00:55:42,340
have found an excuse to set Shir-Ali the butcher

1121
00:55:42,380 --> 00:55:43,030
on him

1122
00:55:43,080 --> 00:55:46,380
and wreck the talks.

1123
00:55:46,420 --> 00:55:49,490
Honestly, I'm afraid Sir will harm himself.

1124
00:55:49,540 --> 00:55:56,000
Remember he talked of Napoleon taking poison after his defeat?

1125
00:55:56,040 --> 00:55:58,850
That's not what worries me.

1126
00:55:58,900 --> 00:56:03,040
If you recall, Napoleon took poison the first time he had to abdicate.

1127
00:56:03,160 --> 00:56:08,060
But the second time he waited until they came and exiled him to Saint Helena.

1128
00:56:09,540 --> 00:56:11,510
I feel so sorry for this old man.

1129
00:56:11,560 --> 00:56:13,690
I'm at my wits' end.

1130
00:56:13,740 --> 00:56:16,200
We'll have to see what happens.

1131
00:56:23,500 --> 00:56:27,470
I've been thinking. What if I had a word with Sardar Maharat-Khan myself?

1132
00:56:27,520 --> 00:56:28,690
About Sir?

1133
00:56:28,740 --> 00:56:32,330
No, about that rug that Indian corporal,

1134
00:56:32,380 --> 00:56:34,600
that charlatan Eshtiaq Khan, pocketed.

1135
00:56:34,640 --> 00:56:37,280
Did I make some vow to give an Isfahan rug

1136
00:56:37,320 --> 00:56:40,105
to that Corporal Eshtiaq Khan?

1137
00:56:40,320 --> 00:56:42,320
In all this, my face ended up like this,

1138
00:56:42,360 --> 00:56:44,780
and I lost my rug too.

1139
00:56:44,820 --> 00:56:47,340
Moment, moment, Colonel.

1140
00:56:47,380 --> 00:56:48,900
Starting a row again?

1141
00:56:48,940 --> 00:56:51,360
Good heavens, Colonel!

1142
00:56:51,400 --> 00:56:53,140
Your brother is falling apart,

1143
00:56:53,180 --> 00:56:54,580
and you're still on about a rug?

1144
00:56:54,620 --> 00:56:56,160
Didn't you say you were ready

1145
00:56:56,200 --> 00:56:58,500
to give your life for your Brother?

1146
00:56:58,540 --> 00:56:59,635
Yes.

1147
00:57:00,300 --> 00:57:02,220
I'm not worried about Brother.

1148
00:57:02,260 --> 00:57:05,240
Brother's no reed to bend in every breeze.

1149
00:57:05,280 --> 00:57:08,620
A man who spent his whole life fighting and struggling

1150
00:57:08,660 --> 00:57:12,540
surely knows how to bear life's ups and downs.

1151
00:57:12,580 --> 00:57:14,325
Besides,

1152
00:57:14,780 --> 00:57:17,500
Shazdeh, this charlatan did nothing for us.

1153
00:57:17,540 --> 00:57:19,420
All right, don't upset yourself.

1154
00:57:19,460 --> 00:57:21,220
Go get the rug back from him.

1155
00:57:21,260 --> 00:57:22,060
He's got a point.

1156
00:57:22,100 --> 00:57:24,520
I don't know where to find this charlatan.

1157
00:57:24,560 --> 00:57:30,640
Let me tell you, Corporal Eshtiaq Khan was due to leave on a mission today.

1158
00:57:30,680 --> 00:57:32,760
Leave? Leave for where?

1159
00:57:32,800 --> 00:57:35,990
He gave me the address. Write it down, write down his address.

1160
00:57:36,040 --> 00:57:37,170
Write it down.

1161
00:57:37,220 --> 00:57:39,480
Write him a letter first thing tomorrow

1162
00:57:39,520 --> 00:57:44,260
to the address I give you, and he'll send your rug right back. There.

1163
00:57:45,700 --> 00:57:47,250
Give me the address.

1164
00:57:47,440 --> 00:57:59,420
Corporal Eshtiaq Khan, Tank 238, El Alamein front, North Africa.

1165
00:58:00,820 --> 00:58:01,890
Got it?

1166
00:58:01,940 --> 00:58:05,500
To hell with your Shazdeh and his ugly mug!

1167
00:58:05,540 --> 00:58:08,240
Yes, sir? What's happened, sir?

